00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.940
Let's see a quick demo
of how this works.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.580
Apigee X provides
self-service capabilities

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.430
to create your own
orgs and environments.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.190
To create an org,
simply click Edit,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.290
select the Analytics
Hosting Region,

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.940
and enter the encryption key.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.750
After configuring the network
and runtime parameters,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.280
you can now create
an environment

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.590
by providing the
required details.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.310
For the purpose of this demo,
I created a Hello World proxy,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.670
added a few developers, and
registered a front-end app.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.490
If I try to access the proxy
without a key, it fails.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.800
To further protect this
proxy with Cloud Armor,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.470
you can create a new rule
or apply an existing rule

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.370
to the API proxy.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.800
When I try to access the same
proxy again without a key,

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.990
you'll see a change in the
error message generated

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:54.880
by Cloud Armor.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.480
Now let's see how to apply Cloud
CDN capabilities to your APIs

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.910
for enhanced caching
and performance.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.010
I created a very
simple CDN policy

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.510
and applied it to
the load balancer.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.820
In addition, I
added cache control

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.850
so that Cloud CDN knows
to cache the responses.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.380
When I access the
proxy from US West,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.060
the call is executed in
about 10 milliseconds.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.890
If I take the same
call and execute it

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:31.090
from a VM located
in Europe West,

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.830
the response time
for the first attempt

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.840
is about 215 milliseconds.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.960
However, because of
the enhanced caching,

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.670
the response time for
the second attempt

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.100
goes down to 16 milliseconds.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.310
